rs6000: save the TOC at each indirect call (cell64lv2)

PRX import stubs store the caller's TOC with a 4-byte stw at 40(r1).
Before Pmode became DImode (0051) the compiler restored the TOC after an
indirect call with a 4-byte lwz from the same word, so a TOC saved once in
the prologue (-msave-toc-indirect, on by default when optimizing for speed)
and then overwritten by a stub with the same value was harmless.  With
Pmode DImode the restore is an 8-byte ld: a function that calls through a
stub and then through a function pointer reloads a TOC whose high word is
the stub's 32-bit copy, and its next TOC-relative access faults
(samples/spurs/sync2-objects: main calls printf, then each row through a
pointer).

The reference compiler saves the TOC with std immediately before every
bctrl, so no call can intervene.  Do the same: under POWERPC_CELL64LV2,
-msave-toc-indirect is off unless given explicitly.

--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.cc
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.cc
@@ -4043,6 +4043,17 @@
       && optimize_function_for_speed_p (cfun))
     rs6000_isa_flags |= OPTION_MASK_SAVE_TOC_INDIRECT;
 
+#ifdef POWERPC_CELL64LV2
+  /* cell64lv2: save the TOC at each indirect call, as the reference
+     toolchain does, never once in the prologue.  A PRX import stub stores
+     the caller's TOC with a 4-byte stw at 40(r1); with Pmode DImode the
+     TOC is restored with an 8-byte ld, so a prologue save followed by a
+     stub call and then an indirect call reloads a TOC whose high word is
+     the stub's copy.  */
+  if ((rs6000_isa_flags_explicit & OPTION_MASK_SAVE_TOC_INDIRECT) == 0)
+    rs6000_isa_flags &= ~OPTION_MASK_SAVE_TOC_INDIRECT;
+#endif
+
   /* Enable power8 fusion if we are tuning for power8, even if we aren't
      generating power8 instructions.  Power9 does not optimize power8 fusion
      cases.  */
